-- Q1: easy / L1 — ASC 260 scope: which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which type of entity is required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["Public entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock", "All entities that prepare GAAP financial statements", "Only entities with annual revenues exceeding $10 million", "Any entity that issues preferred stock"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for public entities — those with common stock or potential common stock traded on a public market. Private entities are not required to present EPS, though they may do so voluntarily. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly limits the requirement to public business entities, not all GAAP preparers.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Post-period stock split retroactive adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Granville Corp. reports basic EPS of $3.00 for the year ended December 31, 2025, based on 200,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $600,000. On February 15, 2026, before the financial statements are issued, Granville declares a 2-for-1 stock split. What effect does the February split have on the 2025 EPS presentation?', '["No adjustment is required because the split occurs after year-end", "Basic EPS must be retroactively adjusted to $1.50 using 400,000 weighted-average shares", "The split is disclosed in the footnotes but EPS remains at $3.00", "Basic EPS is adjusted to $2.25 to reflect six weeks of additional shares in 2026"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance must be given retroactive effect in the EPS computation. The weighted-average shares are adjusted to 400,000 (200,000 x 2), yielding basic EPS of $1.50. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 specifically requires retroactive treatment for post-period stock splits before issuance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Control number for antidilution testing
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the "control number" used to determine whether potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive?', '["Net income less preferred dividends", "Total comprehensive income for the period", "Income from continuing operations less preferred dividends", "Operating income before income taxes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations attributable to the entity (adjusted for preferred dividends) serves as the control number for the antidilution test. A security is antidilutive if its inclusion would increase EPS from continuing operations or decrease the loss per share. Choice A is incorrect because net income includes discontinued operations, which is not the control number specified by ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — RSUs in diluted EPS via treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bellwood Inc. has 500,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $2,000,000. The company granted 20,000 restricted stock units on January 1 with a three-year vesting period. Average unrecognized compensation cost is $300,000 and the average share price is $50. How do the unvested RSUs affect diluted EPS under ASC 260?', '["Excluded from diluted EPS until the RSUs fully vest on the third anniversary", "All 20,000 RSU shares are added to the denominator with no treasury stock offset", "Included only if all vesting conditions are satisfied as of the reporting date", "Treasury stock method applies, adding 14,000 incremental shares after assumed repurchase"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-31, unvested RSUs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds equal the unrecognized compensation cost ($300,000). Shares repurchased at $50 = 6,000, so incremental shares = 20,000 minus 6,000 = 14,000. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires unvested share-based awards to be included in diluted EPS as potential common shares during the vesting period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Shares issued for noncash consideration weighted from issuance date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Haldane Corp. had 100,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On July 1, the company issued 20,000 shares to a consulting firm as payment for advisory services rendered. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["110,000", "120,000", "100,000", "115,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued for noncash consideration are included in the weighted-average from their issuance date. The 20,000 shares issued on July 1 are weighted for six months: (100,000 x 12/12) + (20,000 x 6/12) = 110,000. Choice B (120,000) is incorrect because it includes the full 20,000 shares as if outstanding for the entire year, ignoring the mid-year issuance date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Loss from continuing operations makes all dilutive securities antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Technologies reports a loss from continuing operations of $400,000 and income from discontinued operations of $150,000, resulting in a net loss of $250,000. Meridian has 100,000 weighted-average shares and 10,000 dilutive stock options. How should diluted EPS be reported?', '["Loss from continuing operations $(4.00); net loss $(2.50) — options increase the denominator for both", "Loss from continuing operations $(3.64); net loss $(2.27) — options of 10,000 shares added to denominator", "Loss from continuing operations $(4.00); net loss $(2.27) — options included only for net loss EPS", "Loss from continuing operations $(4.00); net loss $(2.50) — options excluded because they are antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations is the control number for antidilution. Because Meridian reports a loss from continuing operations, including stock options would reduce the loss per share, making them antidilutive. When antidilutive for the control number, options must be excluded from all EPS calculations including net loss. Choice B is incorrect because it includes the options, which would improperly reduce the reported loss per share from continuing operations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Reverse stock split retroactive adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Silverline Inc. had 600,000 common shares outstanding for all of 2025. On March 1, 2025, the company executed a 1-for-3 reverse stock split. What is the weighted-average number of shares for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["400,000", "200,000", "600,000", "333,333"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, reverse stock splits are treated retroactively to the beginning of the earliest period presented, just like forward splits. The 600,000 pre-split shares become 200,000 (600,000 / 3) for the entire year. Choice A (400,000) is incorrect because it improperly weights the pre-split and post-split share counts rather than applying the reverse split retroactively to January 1.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Two-class method: participating preferred earnings allocation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Holdings has 200,000 common shares and 50,000 participating preferred shares that receive a $2 annual dividend and then share equally per share in any remaining distributions. Net income is $500,000, and only the preferred dividends of $100,000 were declared. Under the two-class method in ASC 260, evaluate how undistributed earnings should be allocated and determine basic EPS for common stock.', '["$2.50", "$2.00", "$1.60", "$1.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings to each class based on participation rights. Undistributed earnings = $500,000 minus $100,000 = $400,000. Common share of undistributed: 200,000 / (200,000 + 50,000) x $400,000 = $320,000. Basic EPS = $320,000 / 200,000 = $1.60. Choice B ($2.00) is incorrect because it deducts preferred dividends but fails to allocate the undistributed portion to the participating preferred shares.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — EPS required for each income component
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, for which income components must a public entity present per-share amounts on the face of the income statement?', '["Net income only; no other components require EPS presentation", "Income from continuing operations only, with net income disclosed in notes", "Operating income and net income, each with basic and diluted amounts", "Continuing operations, discontinued operations (if any), and net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, a public entity must present basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. If discontinued operations are reported, per-share amounts for that component must also be presented either on the face or in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires EPS for continuing operations in addition to net income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Written put options: reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Corp. has 400,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $1,600,000. Crestview has outstanding written put options obligating the company to repurchase 10,000 shares at $60 per share. The average market price during the period is $40. What is the effect on diluted EPS?', '["5,000 incremental shares are added to the denominator, reducing diluted EPS", "The put options are antidilutive because the exercise price exceeds the market price and are excluded", "10,000 shares are subtracted from the denominator, increasing diluted EPS", "The written puts have no effect because they have not been exercised"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. The reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity issues shares at market price to fund the repurchase: (10,000 x $60) / $40 = 15,000 shares issued, minus 10,000 repurchased = 5,000 net incremental shares added to the denominator. Choice B is incorrect because unlike call options, written puts are dilutive when exercise price exceeds market price.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted computation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Corp. has net income of $660,000 and 200,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has 4,000 shares of $100 par, 5% convertible preferred stock, each convertible into 5 common shares. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.20", "$3.00", "$2.93", "$3.30"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock adds back preferred dividends (4,000 x $100 x 5% = $20,000) to the numerator and adds the converted shares (4,000 x 5 = 20,000) to the denominator. Diluted EPS = $660,000 / 220,000 = $3.00. The per-share impact ($20,000 / 20,000 = $1.00) is below basic EPS of $3.20, confirming dilution. Choice A ($3.20) is incorrect because it represents basic EPS without considering the convertible preferred.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — Antidilution sequencing with multiple securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Corp. reports income from continuing operations of $800,000 with 200,000 weighted-average common shares (basic EPS $4.00). Northgate has three potentially dilutive securities: (1) stock options adding 8,000 incremental shares with no numerator adjustment, (2) $200,000 face 6% convertible bonds convertible into 10,000 shares with $9,000 after-tax interest, and (3) 2,000 shares of $100 par 5% convertible preferred converting into 8,000 common shares. What is the correct sequencing for diluted EPS inclusion?', '["Options first ($0.90/share), then bonds ($0.00/share), then preferred ($1.25/share)", "Preferred first ($1.25/share), then bonds ($0.90/share), then options ($0.00/share)", "Options first ($0.00/share), then bonds ($0.90/share), then preferred ($1.25/share)", "Bonds first ($0.90/share), then options ($0.00/share), then preferred ($1.25/share)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, potentially dilutive securities must be tested from most to least dilutive based on per-share impact: options at $0.00 (no numerator effect), bonds at $0.90 ($9,000 / 10,000), and preferred at $1.25 ($10,000 / 8,000). Each security is tested sequentially — if adding it increases EPS above the running total, it is antidilutive and excluded. Choice D is incorrect because options with a $0.00 per-share impact are the most dilutive and must be tested first.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Participating securities definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is a participating security?', '["A security that may be converted into common stock at the holder''s option", "A security with a fixed dividend rate that does not share in additional distributions", "A stock option that participates in market appreciation above the exercise price", "A security that may participate in undistributed earnings with common stock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, a participating security is defined as a security that may participate in undistributed earnings with common stock, whether that participation is conditional or unconditional. Common examples include certain preferred stock and restricted stock with nonforfeitable dividend rights. Choice A is incorrect because convertible securities are a separate category; not all convertible securities participate in undistributed earnings.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Share buyback effect on EPS trend analysis
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fenwick Industries reports net income of $2,000,000 in both Year 1 and Year 2. In Year 1, Fenwick had 500,000 weighted-average shares outstanding (basic EPS $4.00). During Year 2, Fenwick repurchased 100,000 shares on January 1, reducing the weighted-average to 400,000. What is the most likely effect of the buyback on the EPS trend?', '["EPS increases from $4.00 to $5.00, reflecting capital allocation rather than improved operating performance", "EPS remains at $4.00 because the repurchased shares are excluded retroactively from Year 1", "EPS decreases because the cash used for repurchases reduces net income", "EPS increases to $5.00, reflecting genuine improvement in per-share profitability"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, share repurchases reduce the weighted-average denominator from the repurchase date. With identical net income of $2,000,000, EPS rises from $4.00 to $5.00 ($2,000,000 / 400,000). However, this increase reflects the capital allocation decision to repurchase shares, not improved operating performance. Choice D is incorrect because the higher EPS is purely mechanical — the numerator is unchanged, so no improvement in profitability occurred.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Complex capital structure: dual EPS presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which EPS figures must a public entity with a complex capital structure present on the face of the income statement?', '["Basic EPS only, with diluted EPS disclosed in footnotes", "Both basic EPS and diluted EPS with equal prominence", "Diluted EPS only, since it subsumes basic EPS information", "Basic EPS, diluted EPS, and adjusted EPS for each component"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities with complex capital structures — those with potentially dilutive securities — must present both basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement with equal prominence. An entity with a simple capital structure may present only basic EPS. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 requires basic EPS in all cases; diluted EPS supplements but does not replace it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Convertible debt issued mid-year: if-converted from issuance date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Barrett Technologies has net income of $1,800,000 and 400,000 weighted-average common shares for 2025. On July 1, 2025, Barrett issues $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds at par, each $1,000 bond convertible into 25 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. The bonds remain outstanding at year-end. The controller assumes conversion at January 1, adding $45,000 after-tax interest and 25,000 shares. Evaluate the controller''s approach.', '["The controller''s method is correct — the if-converted method always assumes conversion at January 1", "The controller overstated the interest add-back but correctly used 25,000 incremental shares", "The controller should assume conversion at July 1, adding $22,500 after-tax interest and 12,500 weighted shares", "The controller should exclude the bonds entirely because they were not outstanding the full year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when convertible securities are issued during the period, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the issuance date, not the beginning of the period. The correct adjustment is six months of after-tax interest ($1,000,000 x 6% x 6/12 x 0.75 = $22,500) and 25,000 shares weighted for six months (12,500). Choice A is incorrect because assuming conversion at January 1 applies only to securities outstanding at the start of the year.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Warrants: treasury stock method calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Corp. has 350,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $1,050,000. The company has 15,000 outstanding warrants with an exercise price of $30 per share. The average market price during the period was $50. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares do the warrants add to diluted EPS?', '["15,000", "9,000", "3,000", "6,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, warrants are treated identically to stock options under the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds ($450,000 = 15,000 x $30) are used to repurchase shares at the average market price ($450,000 / $50 = 9,000). Incremental shares = 15,000 minus 9,000 = 6,000. Choice A (15,000) is incorrect because it ignores the treasury stock method''s assumed repurchase, which offsets a portion of the dilutive effect.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Two-class method purpose and definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the purpose of the two-class method for computing basic EPS under ASC 260?', '["To compute separate EPS figures for basic and diluted presentations", "To allocate net income between continuing and discontinued operations", "To distribute earnings between domestic and foreign operations", "To allocate undistributed earnings between common stock and participating securities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, the two-class method allocates earnings to each class of common stock and participating security based on dividend rights and participation in undistributed earnings. It is required when an entity has securities that participate in dividends alongside common stock. Choice A is incorrect because the two-class method applies specifically to basic EPS allocation among equity classes, not to the basic versus diluted distinction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Discontinued operations: same diluted denominator for all components
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hargrove Industries has 450,000 weighted-average common shares, income from continuing operations of $2,250,000, and a loss from discontinued operations of $450,000 (net of tax). Hargrove has dilutive stock options adding 50,000 incremental shares under the treasury stock method. Evaluate the correct diluted EPS presentation for each income component.', '["Diluted EPS uses 500,000 shares for continuing operations only; options are excluded from discontinued operations", "Diluted EPS uses 500,000 shares for continuing operations and net income, but 450,000 for discontinued operations", "Diluted EPS uses 500,000 shares for all components: continuing $4.50, discontinued ($0.90), net income $3.60", "EPS for discontinued operations is not required; only continuing operations and net income need per-share amounts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-2, the same diluted share count (500,000) must be used for all EPS components within a period. The dilution test uses income from continuing operations as the control number; once options are determined to be dilutive for that amount, the same incremental shares apply to discontinued operations and net income. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 prohibits using different denominators for different income components within the same period.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Rights offering bonus element and adjustment factor
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornbridge Corp. has 100,000 common shares outstanding and issues rights allowing shareholders to purchase 1 new share for every 5 held at $20 per share. The market price before the offering is $30. Under ASC 260-10-55-18, what is the effect of this below-market rights offering on EPS calculations?', '["No adjustment is needed because rights offerings are accounted for prospectively from the exercise date", "An adjustment factor based on the theoretical ex-rights price is applied to shares outstanding before the offering, similar to a stock dividend", "Only the new shares issued are added to the weighted-average from the offering date with no retroactive adjustment", "The rights are treated as stock options using the treasury stock method for diluted EPS only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-18, a rights offering priced below market contains a bonus element. An adjustment factor is computed as the fair value per share before exercise divided by the theoretical ex-rights price ($30 / $28.33 = 1.059). This factor is applied to shares outstanding before the offering, similar to a stock dividend. Choice D is incorrect because the bonus element in a below-market rights offering requires retroactive adjustment of weighted-average shares, not the treasury stock method.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Stock dividends retroactive to earliest period presented
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, how should a stock dividend be treated when computing weighted-average shares outstanding across comparative periods?', '["Weighted from the declaration date in the current period only", "Weighted from the record date in the current period only", "Weighted from the distribution date in the current period only", "Applied retroactively to the beginning of the earliest period presented"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends and stock splits are given retroactive treatment in the EPS computation, applied as if they occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented in the financial statements. This ensures comparability across periods. Choice A is incorrect because stock dividends are not weighted from the declaration date; they are applied retroactively, unlike shares issued for cash or other consideration.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Simple capital structure requires only basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what EPS presentation is required for a public entity with a simple capital structure?', '["Basic EPS only, with no diluted EPS presentation required", "Diluted EPS only, since it provides more useful information", "Both basic and diluted EPS presented with equal prominence", "Pro forma EPS based on potential common share issuances"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, an entity with a simple capital structure — one with no outstanding securities that could potentially dilute EPS — presents only basic EPS on the face of the income statement. Diluted EPS is required only when potentially dilutive securities exist, creating a complex capital structure. Choice C is incorrect because dual presentation of basic and diluted EPS applies only to entities with complex capital structures, not simple ones.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L2 — Treasury shares excluded from weighted-average
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hillcrest Corp. has 500,000 shares of common stock issued and 40,000 shares held in treasury for the entire year. Net income is $1,840,000 with no preferred stock. What is basic EPS?', '["$3.68", "$3.83", "$4.00", "$4.09"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury shares are excluded from the weighted-average shares outstanding. With 500,000 issued shares minus 40,000 treasury shares held all year, the weighted-average is 460,000. Basic EPS = $1,840,000 / 460,000 = $4.00. Choice A ($3.68) is incorrect because it uses all 500,000 issued shares as the denominator, failing to exclude treasury stock from the computation.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Full diluted EPS with multiple securities and sequencing
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Corp. reports income from continuing operations of $1,500,000 with 300,000 weighted-average shares. The company has: (1) 20,000 stock options adding 5,000 incremental shares, (2) $400,000 of 10% convertible bonds (400 bonds, 20 shares each, 25% tax rate), and (3) 8,000 shares of $50 par 6% convertible preferred (3 common shares per preferred). After proper sequencing, determine diluted EPS.', '["$4.92", "$4.61", "$4.89", "$5.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, securities are sequenced by per-share impact: options ($0.00), preferred ($1.00 = $24,000 / 24,000 shares), then bonds ($3.75 = $30,000 / 8,000 shares). Including all three yields numerator $1,554,000 and denominator 337,000 for diluted EPS of $4.61. Each security is dilutive at its step. Choice C ($4.89) is incorrect because it omits the convertible preferred stock from the diluted calculation, improperly excluding a dilutive security.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Forward purchase contract: reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Maplewood Inc. has net income of $2,400,000 and 600,000 weighted-average common shares. Maplewood entered a forward contract to repurchase 20,000 of its own shares at $75 per share. The average market price during the period is $50. Under the reverse treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to diluted EPS?', '["20,000 incremental shares", "30,000 incremental shares", "10,000 incremental shares", "Zero — the contract is antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, forward repurchase contracts at above-market prices are dilutive. Using the reverse treasury stock method, the entity assumes it issues shares at market price to fund the repurchase: (20,000 x $75) / $50 = 30,000 shares issued, minus 20,000 repurchased = 10,000 net incremental shares. Choice D is incorrect because the contract is dilutive when the forward price ($75) exceeds the average market price ($50).', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Stock compensation expense effect on basic EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redfield Corp. has 250,000 weighted-average common shares and revenues of $5,000,000 with total expenses of $3,800,000, including $200,000 of share-based compensation expense under ASC 718. Net income is $1,200,000. How does the stock compensation expense affect basic EPS?', '["Stock compensation expense does not affect basic EPS because it is a noncash charge", "Stock compensation expense is added back to net income for basic EPS, similar to depreciation in cash flow", "Stock compensation expense reduces EPS by increasing the denominator through assumed share issuances", "Stock compensation expense reduces net income and therefore reduces the basic EPS numerator, yielding $4.80"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, basic EPS uses reported net income as the numerator. Stock compensation expense under ASC 718 reduces net income like any other expense, directly lowering the basic EPS numerator. Basic EPS = $1,200,000 / 250,000 = $4.80. Choice A is incorrect because although stock compensation is noncash, it is recognized in the income statement and reduces net income, which is the numerator for basic EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Shares from option exercise weighted from exercise date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Alderton Corp. had 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On September 1, employees exercised 12,000 stock options, and Alderton issued 12,000 new shares. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["304,000", "312,000", "300,000", "306,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued upon exercise of stock options are included in the weighted-average from the exercise date. The 12,000 new shares are weighted for four months (September through December): (300,000 x 8/12) + (312,000 x 4/12) = 200,000 + 104,000 = 304,000. Choice B (312,000) is incorrect because it treats the exercised shares as outstanding for the entire year rather than from the September 1 exercise date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Interim year-to-date EPS computation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brookhaven Corp. had 200,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On April 1, the company issued 100,000 additional shares. Net income was $300,000 for Q1 and $300,000 for Q2. Under ASC 260, what is the year-to-date basic EPS through the second quarter?', '["$2.50", "$2.40", "$1.25", "$2.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-57, interim year-to-date EPS uses cumulative net income ($600,000) divided by the year-to-date weighted-average shares: (200,000 x 3/6) + (300,000 x 3/6) = 250,000. Year-to-date EPS = $600,000 / 250,000 = $2.40. Choice A ($2.50) is incorrect because it sums the quarterly EPS figures ($1.50 + $1.00), which is not the proper computation — EPS must be calculated on a year-to-date basis, not by summing quarterly amounts.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Performance conditions on share awards and diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Vanguard Corp. granted 30,000 performance-based stock options on January 1, 2025. The options vest only if cumulative revenue exceeds $50 million over three years. As of December 31, 2025, cumulative revenue is $14 million, making it not probable the target will be met. How do these options affect diluted EPS for 2025?', '["The options are included in diluted EPS because they have been granted regardless of vesting probability", "The options are included based on the proportion of the service period completed (one-third of shares)", "The options are excluded from diluted EPS because the performance condition is not satisfied at the reporting date", "The options are included at their maximum potential dilution of 30,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, share-based payment awards with performance conditions are included in diluted EPS only if the conditions would be satisfied assuming the reporting date is the end of the contingency period. Since the revenue target is not met as of December 31, 2025, the options are excluded from diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because performance-based awards are contingently issuable shares — they are not automatically included merely because they have been granted.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred classified as liability
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Corp. has net income of $900,000 and 300,000 weighted-average common shares. Waverly issued 10,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock at $50 par with a 7% dividend rate. Under ASC 480, these shares are classified as liabilities, and the annual dividends of $35,000 were recognized as interest expense in net income. Evaluate whether the preferred dividends should be deducted from the EPS numerator.', '["No deduction is needed because the dividends are already recognized as interest expense in net income; basic EPS is $3.00", "The $35,000 must still be deducted because all preferred dividends reduce the EPS numerator regardless of classification", "The $35,000 should be added back because mandatorily redeemable preferred is treated as debt; basic EPS is $3.12", "Basic EPS cannot be determined without knowing whether the preferred stock is cumulative or noncumulative"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 480-10-25, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability, and its dividends are recognized as interest expense — already reducing net income. Under ASC 260-10-45-11, only dividends on equity-classified preferred stock are deducted from the EPS numerator. Since the $35,000 is already reflected as expense, no further deduction is needed: $900,000 / 300,000 = $3.00. Choice B is incorrect because it double-counts the preferred payment by deducting it from both net income and the EPS numerator.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
